From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Merchant <john@johnmerchant.dev>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 04:53:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 44/44] hw/misc/chumby_io: Plug in a USB keyboard when a key
 goes unheard

The window's keys go to the board's top-button handler, which passes on
the rest to the guest's keyboard; without one they were dropped, and a
user had to start the machine with -device usb-kbd to type.

Plug a usb-kbd into the root hub instead when a key is pressed that no
keyboard takes, as a user would plug one in to type ("usb-keyboard", on
by default). The port's reset empties the keyboard's queue, so the keys
are held until the guest has configured the keyboard and its driver has
had half a second to open it, then typed one every 20 ms: the keyboard
queues only 16 key changes.

A keyboard, plugged in or given with -device, puts its handler first;
put the button's back in front, so its key still holds the button.

Linux 7.2.13 with usbhid built in: "echo hi", typed before the keyboard
was there, runs on the screen's shell once it is.

Signed-off-by: John Merchant <john@johnmerchant.dev>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01B1fN5Fc77Z6fVVe4rWJxPT
